# Lesson 6, Mike's Exercise 3, turned so the student (South) is on lead. East deals here, so dummy's jump raise is an unpassed hand. South's cards are Mike's; the other hands were re-dealt 2026-11-27 so good defense beats it.
title: Your Exercise 3: a singleton
id: L06-06
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.8
student: S
board: 6

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  J      964    KT85    AT732
  E  K9854  AKT8   J       K94
  S  T72    J532   A9762   6
  W  AQ63   Q7     Q43     QJ85

auction:
  E 1S     = 13+ points, 4+ spades
  S Pass   = Not enough to bid
  W 3S     = An opening hand or better with 4+ spades: game is on
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 4S     = Game in spades
  S Pass   = Nothing to add
  W Pass   = Done
  N Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1♠ and West's jump to 3♠ showed an opening hand with four or
  more spades, so East bid the game. East is declarer, and so you, South,
  will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 13-21 = East opened one of a suit: 13 to 21.
  dummy: 13-27 = West's jump raise showed an opening hand or better.
  partner: 0-9 = 40, minus your 5 and the opponents' 26 or more, leaves at most 9.
  remind: You hold three trumps. Could a short suit turn one of them into a trick?

play:
  1   S C6*  W C5  N CA  E C4
      C6 key: A singleton in a side suit. If partner can win a club, you ruff the next one: a cheap trick from your small trumps.
      C6 not D: Leading away from an ace against a suit contract can give declarer a trick with the king. Your ♦A is a sure trick: save it.
      C6 not DA: Cashing the ♦A is safe, but it builds nothing. Your singleton club could earn a ruff.
      C6 not H: From J532 a heart is a guess. Your singleton club has a plan.
      C6 not S: Your small trumps are worth more ruffing clubs than leading out.
      note: Partner wins the ♣A. She has five clubs and can see four in dummy, so she can guess your ♣6 may be a singleton.
  2   N CT  E C9  S S2  W C8
      note: Partner returns a club, and you ruff.
  3   S D2  W D3  N DK  E DJ
      note: How can you reach partner again for a second ruff? Dummy has the ♦Q, so partner may hold the ♦K. Lead a low diamond, not the ace: cash the ace first and declarer ruffs the next diamond. Partner's king wins.
  4   N C7  E CK  S S7  W CJ
      note: A second ruff, and declarer's ♣K goes with it. Four tricks for the defense: down one.
  5   S ST  W S3  N SJ  E SK
      note: Your ♦A would be ruffed now, so you get out with your last trump.
  6   E HA  S H2  W H7  N H4
      note: Declarer takes the rest.
  7   E HK  S H3  W HQ  N H6
  8   E S9  S H5  W S6  N C2
  9   E S8  S D6  W SQ  N C3
  10  W CQ  N D5  E H8  S D7
  11  W D4  N D8  E S4  S D9
  12  E HT  S HJ  W SA  N H9
  13  W DQ  N DT  E S5  S DA

play recap:
  Your singleton club found partner's ♣A, and the first ruff came at once.
  The low diamond was the clever part: partner's ♦K got her in again, and
  your second ruff was the setting trick. Cash the ♦A first, and declarer
  ruffs the diamond return. Any other opening lead lets declarer draw
  trumps and make 4♠. A singleton lead needs two things: a partner who can
  win tricks, and trumps of your own to ruff with before declarer draws
  them.
